  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(t)$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "By Newton\u2019s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-1}] \\\\  & =-{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
